# **Truncation has a name**, and the name carries the destination
# ([ADR-043](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-043.md) D7). Since D4 a conversion
# that may not fit aborts, so this is the only way left to say "keep the low
# digits" - said rather than assumed, exactly as `wrapping_` is said.
#
# One entry per destination, because that is what a signature can be written for:
# `truncating_i32` hands back an `i32` whatever it was given, and a single name
# with a type that follows the call site is not something the ledger can say.
#
# The sources are the two a Nikaia program can have a narrowing conversion out
# of: the larger integer and a floating-point number. There is no
# `truncating_f64` - nothing narrows *to* one in a program written in the types
# Part I 2.2 offers - and there is no `truncating_usize` either, since ADR-048 D1:
# a length is an `i64`, the machine-width type left the surface a program can
# write, and nothing narrows out of a type nobody can hold.
#
# `touches = []` and `sync = true` for the reason the arithmetic entries give: a
# pure computation that reaches no resource.

[fn."i64::truncating_i32"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64) -> i32"
doc = "The low 32 bits of an `i64`, as an `i32`.\n\nA conversion that may not fit aborts, so this is how a program says it means\nto keep the low digits - said rather than assumed."

[fn."f64::truncating_i32"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(f64) -> i32"
doc = "The whole part of an `f64`, as an `i32`, with the digits that do not fit\ndropped.\n\nA conversion that may not fit aborts, so this is how a program says it means\nto keep the low digits."

[fn."f64::truncating_i64"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(f64) -> i64"
doc = "The whole part of an `f64`, as an `i64`, with the digits that do not fit\ndropped.\n\nA conversion that may not fit aborts, so this is how a program says it means\nto keep the low digits."

[fn."collections::HashMap::entry"]
pub = true
sync = true
mutates = true
touches = []
keeps = ["key"]
signature = "(ref collections::HashMap[$K, $V], key: ?) -> Entry[$V]"
doc = "The place in the map this key names, whether or not anything is there yet.\n\nOne lookup rather than two: `or_insert` puts a value in where there is none,\nand `and_modify` changes the one that is. The map **keeps** the key."

# `key: ?` and not `key: $K`, deliberately. Rust's `get` takes anything the key
# borrows as, so `m.get("x")` on a `HashMap[String, …]` is correct and `$K`
# would reject it. The rule is ADR-031's: a variable says what flows *out*, and
# `?` stays for what flows *in*.
# **A key may not be there**, which is what `-> $V` used to deny.
#
# It was not a thin entry but a **wrong** one, and the difference shows in what
# it cost: with `get` claiming a `$V`, `counts.get(k)?.n` - the honest Nikaia
# spelling - was refused as `NK1121` *"reaches through a `Tally`, which cannot
# be absent"*, while `counts.get(k).map_or(0, …)` compiled by writing Rust's
# `Option` surface through a hole. So the ledger refused the correct program and
# accepted the one this language has its own words for, which is Part III C.4's
# rule standing on its head.
[fn."collections::HashMap::get"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(ref collections::HashMap[$K, $V], key: ?) -> $V?"
doc = "What this key names, or nothing where the key is not there.\n\nA key is data and may be absent, which is why this hands back a `$V?`: the\nshape a program writes is `counts.get(k) ?? 0`, or `counts.get(k)?.field`."

# **Written into, by name** ([ADR-215](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-215.md)
# D1): `m[k] = v` is this language's form, and this is the same write under the
# name the language below gives it, for a program that wants the value it
# replaced. The map keeps both, so both are handed over (ADR-213 D3).
[fn."collections::HashMap::insert"]
pub = true
sync = true
mutates = true
touches = []
keeps = ["key", "value"]
signature = "(ref collections::HashMap[$K, $V], key: $K, value: $V) -> $V?"
doc = "Put a value in the map under this key, and hand back the one it replaced, or\nnothing where the key was not there.\n\nThe map keeps both the key and the value."

[fn."collections::HashMap::keys"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(ref collections::HashMap[$K, $V]) -> Seq[ref $K] sync sized"
doc = "The keys, one at a time.\n\nA map has no order of its own, so a program that prints one says which order\nit means."

[fn."collections::HashMap::len"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(ref collections::HashMap[?, ?]) -> i64"
doc = "How many pairs the map holds."

[fn."collections::HashMap::new"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "() -> collections::HashMap[?, ?]"
doc = "A map with nothing in it. It is written `HashMap()`."

# **Whether a key is there**, without the value. Observed being asked for: a
