# **An array knows its own length, and nothing said so.** `Array[T, N]` carries
# its length in its type (ADR-152), and below it is `[T; N]`, which has `.len()`
# and `.is_empty()` the same as a slice does. Without these entries `xs.len()`
# on one resolved to nothing — which lowered and ran, and cost the *enclosing*
# function its touch set, because an unresolved call is unknown (ADR-033). The
# visible price was a build-time body: `for i in 0..<xs.len()` made `squares`
# uncallable from a `comptime` with *nothing says what it touches*, while the
# same loop written `0..<5` was fine.
# **A map whose keys the build knew** (ADR-176). Written as a list of pairs
# under a `comptime`, and what it crosses as is four static tables — no
# allocation, no generated code, and the lookup shape decided from the key count
# while the program was built.
#
# `Fixed` is keyed **with no module in front**, which is `Overtaken`'s and
# `ParseError`'s shape: a program writes it in a declaration and never reaches
# for a path to it.
[type."Fixed"]
pub = true
crosses = true
doc = "A map whose keys were known while the program was built.\n\nWritten as a list of pairs — `comptime ROUTES: Fixed[ref String, i64] = [(\"get\", 1)]` —\nand crossed as static tables. There is nothing to build one from at run time:\nwhat makes it fixed is that the key set closed at the build."

